Fixes #134.

`Vault.registrable` took a host's last two labels as its site, plus a short list of two-part country endings. `logins(matching:)` offers every saved login whose host shares that "site", so:

- a password saved on `alice.github.io` was offered on `evil.github.io`, and the same for `herokuapp.com`, `blogspot.com`, `web.app` and `s3.amazonaws.com` siblings;
- IP addresses too, which #134 doesn't mention: `192.168.1.1` and `10.0.1.1` both became "1.1", so a router password saved at home was offered to `10.0.1.1` on any other network.

### What changes
As #134 suggests, sites are now told apart with the public suffix list macOS keeps, through the same `_CFHostIsDomainTopLevel` test the passkey checks already use (`Passkeys.publicSuffix`, now `nonisolated` so `Vault` can reach it).

- `Registrable.domain(of:isSuffix:)` (new, Foundation only) returns the longest public suffix inside the host plus one label: `accounts.example.com` → `example.com`, `news.bbc.co.uk` → `bbc.co.uk`, `docs.alice.github.io` → `alice.github.io`.
- It returns the host itself when:
  - it's an IP address (v4 or v6);
  - it's a single label, like `localhost`;
  - it's itself on the list, like `blob.core.windows.net`, which is otherwise grouped with `windows.net`;
  - no suffix of it is on the list, like `nas.local`;
  - macOS's test isn't available.
- In every one of those cases matching falls back to the exact host, never the old guess.
- `Vault.registrable` now calls it. The doc comment's last sentence, "a full public suffix list would be a library for a corner", is no longer true and is removed.

The case the matching exists for still works: `accounts.example.com` is offered the password kept for `example.com`.

### Testing
- 39 checks, run with `swiftc` against the new file and **macOS's real public-suffix data**, phrased as "is a password saved on A offered on B?", the same comparison `logins(matching:)` makes. They cover:
  - shared-hosting siblings;
  - a site and its own subdomains;
  - IPv4 and IPv6, `.local`, single labels, trailing dots and uppercase;
  - wildcard and exception entries (`x.b.ck`, `www.ck`), EC2 hosts, punycode, and Azure's `core.windows.net` suffixes;
  - no system test available.
- The checks were watched failing against a stub first. I left them out of the PR because the repo has no test target.
- The old rule, run on the same pairs, reproduces every leak above.
- Not exercised: the suggestion list in the app end to end, because test profiles keep their passwords in the real login keychain.
- `swift build`: no new warnings.

Search 1.0.3 (build 202609241746), macOS 27.2, Tampermonkey 5.5.0 from the Chrome Web Store.

**What I did:** installed a user script for GitHub, then opened github.com.

**Expected:** the script works, as it does in Chrome.

**What happens:** the script does nothing. Tampermonkey's menu shows it as enabled on the page, and there is no error.

It seems to depend on the site. The same kind of script works on sites like example.com and wikipedia.org, but not on github.com or developer.mozilla.org. Those two sites send a strict Content-Security-Policy, so that may be related.

A three-line script is enough to see it:

```js
// ==UserScript==
// @name  CSP probe
// @match https://github.com/*
// @grant none
// ==/UserScript==
document.documentElement.dataset.probe = 'ran';
```

On github.com, `document.documentElement.dataset.probe` stays empty.
